# etude-ldf plan — `etude doctor`

ANCESTOR: etude-ldf (P1) — operators need a read-only, CI-gateable diagnosis before legacy refspecs or setup drift lose run records. Measurement: an earlier configured fetch/prune deleted three gate attempts, and the first plan gate found eight concrete false-OK or false-remediation modes.

BLAST RADIUS: worktree and this repository only. The command performs filesystem
and Git reads, including a sanitized, bounded `git ls-remote`; its own code writes
no repository, ref, config, remote, cache, credential, or host state. Verification
installs only below `.etude/tmp`, not the user or system Go bin directory. Gate
packets overwrite one path under `.etude/tmp`.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: none

TOPOLOGY: linked worktrees share one Git common directory and ref store;
configured remotes and other lanes may change config or refs during diagnosis.
Doctor takes conservative ordered reads and never claims a transactional snapshot.

DURABILITY / DELIVERY CLASS: doctor produces terminal output and an exit code only;
it creates no durable record, cache, ref, config edit, worktree file, or remote
write. A concurrent change may cause a conservative WARN. Git cannot provide an
atomic cross-remote snapshot without locking, so doctor states that limitation
rather than promising linearizability it cannot provide.

AUTHORITATIVE-vs-PROXY: Git config, local refs, and the remote advertisement are
the authoritative sources for their respective checks. Filesystem/PATH presence
is explicitly only a `PROXY` for reviewer-seat operability and can never become
an OK, authentication claim, or exit gate.

COMPLETENESS-ENUMERATION: the complete checked set is every configured remote;
each ref kind in `refstore.Kinds` (runs, retros, evals); every workflow stage,
default runner, stage runner, gate check, inline gate seat, and tier reference;
every registry seat primary and invocation fallback; every rubric path; every
repo-relative command path; and every local etude run/retro/eval ref compared
against every configured remote. The tests enumerate each set and include a
counterexample for a missed member.

TRUST BOUNDARY: repository YAML, Git config values, remote names/advertisements,
ref names, and invocation strings are untrusted input. They are never evaluated
by a shell. Repository-supplied transport, credential, replacement-ref, and lazy-
fetch configuration is neutralized. Installed Git and standard transport binaries
are trusted executables; malicious replacements are outside scope. Git receives
option-terminated fixed argv for read-only subcommands; rendered remedies are data
escaped for later operator copy/paste. Reviewer commands are parsed and probed but
never executed.

## Goal and contract

Add a registered `etude doctor` command that runs from an etude Git repository,
prints one result per health area, and exits non-zero when any result is `FAIL`.
`WARN` does not fail. Seat executability uses a fourth deliberately un-greenable
`PROXY` label: even a found binary does not prove authentication, quota, or tier
eligibility, so this check can never claim `OK` or affect the exit status.

Every actionable finding emits an exact, runnable, shell-quoted command only when
that command is sufficient for the diagnosed state. When contents or operator
intent cannot be derived (a missing rubric, malformed cross-reference, an absent
reviewer binary, or a broad user-owned fetch mapping), output instead says exactly
what is missing, what property the repair must satisfy, and `HUMAN AUTHORSHIP
REQUIRED`. Never print a placeholder or a plausible command that cannot fix the
finding.

## Design

### 1. Read-only runner and output model

Register `newDoctorCommand` in `internal/cli/root.go` and implement the runner in
`internal/cli/doctor.go`. Resolve the repository root once. All Git access goes
through a doctor-local allowlisted read helper whose accepted subcommands are
only `remote`, `config --get-all`, `ls-remote`, `for-each-ref`, `cat-file -e`, and
`merge-base --is-ancestor`; reject any other argv at the call site. Do not use
`fetch`, `config --unset`, `update-ref`, or a cache. Accumulate findings, render
deterministically, and return a sentinel error only after every independent check
has reported so one failure does not hide the rest.

Remote/config topology is a sequence of observations, not a transaction. Doctor
does not promise a coherent cross-remote snapshot that Git cannot provide without
a write lock. Enumerate a remote, read all of its config, read its remote refs
before local refs, and re-read the relevant config values afterward. A detected
change reports `WARN: repository changed during doctor; rerun`. An undetectable
ABA or another remote changing outside its observation window is an explicit
limitation in output and docs. Remote-first makes local creation conservative; a
concurrent push can still cause a false WARN, which is acceptable because WARN
never gates.

Every remote read has a finite context timeout and noninteractive sanitized
environment: `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`, `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`,
`GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1`, `GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS=1`, credential helpers disabled,
`protocol.ext.allow=never`, and SSH batch/no-control/no-known-host-write options.
Resolve the configured URL as data, reject `ext::` and custom helpers without
invoking them, and call `ls-remote --end-of-options <url> refs/etude/*` so an
option-like remote or URL cannot become syntax. Timeout or unsupported transport
is `FAIL` with human remediation, never a hang or an OK.

### 2. Refspec parsing and safety

Extend the existing `refspec` representation with strict syntax validation:
optional leading `+`; recognize a leading `^` negative fetch refspec as a valid,
destination-less exclusion that creates no local ref and never supplies positive
coverage; at most one colon for positive mappings; non-empty legal sides where
required; and Git's single-wildcard pairing rules. A colonless fetch has no
destination and is harmless. Implement single-`*` pattern intersection/mapping
helpers rather than trailing-prefix matching, so partial globs such as
`refs/*/unpushed:refs/*/unpushed` are evaluated correctly.

For every remote:

- `FAIL` each valid fetch mapping whose destination is explicitly under the
  authoritative `refs/etude/{runs,retros,evals}/` namespace. Emit one surgical
  command per exact offending value. Read effective values with config origin and
  scope metadata. For local/worktree/global scope, emit the matching scoped
  `git config <scope> --unset-all <quoted-key> <quoted-^ERE$>` command; for an
  included or otherwise ambiguous origin, emit an exact `--file <origin>` command
  only when the origin is a writable filesystem path, else require human editing.
  Never emit `--local` for an inherited value and never remove all key values.
- `WARN` for a broader user-owned destination pattern that intersects an
  authoritative etude kind without being an etude-owned mapping. Do not suggest
  deleting it; require a human to narrow the mapping or adopt an operating rule
  that ensures refs are synchronized before prune.
- `FAIL` malformed push/fetch entries that prevent a safe conclusion. An invalid
  exact value may have a surgical unset command, but no replacement is fabricated.
- Evaluate push coverage per kind using concrete probe refs and the refspec as a
  mapping. Coverage may be supplied by one namespace-wide mapping or multiple
  name-preserving per-kind mappings. Empty-source deletes, single-ref mappings,
  sub-namespace globs, and name-changing mappings never count as complete. If any
  kind is uncovered, `FAIL` with `etude init --remote <quoted-remote>`; also name
  the observed misleading shapes so the operator knows why they did not count.
- Treat effective `remote.<name>.mirror=true` as broad name-preserving push
  coverage for every etude kind, and state that coverage comes from mirror-push
  semantics rather than a push refspec.
- Check safe fetch mirroring per kind. Treat the operator-specified mirrored
  layout `refs/etude/remotes/<remote>/<kind>/*` as safe, treat the landed
  33fd4bf sibling layout produced by current `etude init`
  (`refs/etude-mirror/<remote>/<kind>/*`) as safe, and reject the old single
  authoritative namespace as hazardous. Missing safe mirrors are `WARN` with
  `etude init --remote <quoted-remote>`. This compatibility rule prevents doctor
  from diagnosing either explicitly safe mirror layout as the legacy data-loss
  state while remaining correct for the checked-out implementation.

All interpolated values use a POSIX single-quote helper that handles embedded
single quotes. `git config --unset-all` patterns use `regexp.QuoteMeta` plus
`^...$` before shell quoting. No angle-bracket or Git-accepted fake placeholder
is emitted.

### 3. Config parsing, cross-resolution, and referenced paths

Read `.etude/workflow.yaml` and `.etude/registry.yaml` with their strict package
parsers. Missing both files may recommend `etude init`; malformed or partially
missing authored config is `FAIL` with a precise `HUMAN AUTHORSHIP REQUIRED`
statement rather than `etude init --force` (which could destroy intent).

After parse, verify every workflow default/stage/check runner name and every
inline gate seat resolves to `registry.Seats`, and every `gate.tier` resolves to
`registry.Tiers`; registry parsing already proves each tier's seats exist. Report
all unresolved references, not only the first.

Path bases match runtime behavior and are printed explicitly:

- eval rubric paths resolve beneath `.etude/`, the workflow configuration root;
- repo-relative command paths in workflow runners/checks and registry primary or
  fallback invocations resolve beneath the repository root;
- absolute paths remain absolute;
- bare executable names are seat/runner reachability concerns, not filesystem
  path references.

A rubric must resolve to a readable regular file. A missing, directory, or
unreadable rubric is `FAIL` and says it must contain the evaluation criteria for
its named stage, marked `HUMAN AUTHORSHIP REQUIRED`. A missing referenced script,
directory, or non-executable file is `FAIL` and says which invocation requires a
regular executable at which exact resolved path, also human-authored unless an
existing exact generator command is known.

### 4. Seat reachability proxy

Do not run a review or authenticate. For every registry seat primary/fallback,
workflow default/stage runner, and gate check command, tokenize exactly as runtime
does (`strings.Fields`), then resolve through known prefixes:

- interpret an `env` prefix rather than skipping it: honor `-i`, `-u NAME`,
  `--unset=NAME`, `--chdir DIR`, and `NAME=value`; compute effective PATH and cwd
  before resolving the real command;
- resolve the first program using that environment/cwd, recognize an adapter by
  its resolved executable basename `seat-adapter.sh` (including relative variants
  such as `./scripts/seat-adapter.sh`), require a regular executable file, then
  probe the reviewer command after the adapter's seat-name token;
- classify `in-harness:` candidates as host-provided proxies without pretending
  they are PATH executables;
- for repo-relative executables, resolve from repo root; for bare executables,
  use `exec.LookPath` on the resolved reviewer token, never token zero.

Present binaries print `PROXY` with their resolved path and an explicit statement
that auth/quota/tier were not tested. Missing binaries print `WARN` and
`HUMAN AUTHORSHIP REQUIRED: install <binary> and make it available on PATH`;
there is no portable install command to fabricate. This proxy never controls the
process exit code.

### 5. Unpushed refs

For each configured remote, run one sanitized bounded `git ls-remote` against its
resolved URL first, then enumerate local `refs/etude/{runs,retros,evals}/*` once.
Absent remote refs and local-ahead refs are `WARN` with the exact
`etude sync --remote <quoted-remote>` command only when pushing/synchronizing is
the correct next action. A local ref proven behind the remote must instead say to
fetch/compare and must not recommend pushing. If the remote OID is unavailable
locally, the repository is shallow, or both ancestry directions are false, report
direction `UNKNOWN` and require comparison; do not call it diverged or recommend
a push. Disable replacement refs and lazy promisor fetches for ancestry probes so
the result matches remote-visible history and cannot fetch objects. `ls-remote`
failure/timeout is `FAIL` because doctor cannot safely claim refs are pushed, with
a human-authorship statement to repair remote reachability/auth.

